{
  "project": {
    "name": "waydroid-container",
    "version": "0.1.0",
    "description": "Waydroid (official LineageOS-based vendor Android image) run headless inside a Docker container, decoupled from the live desktop Wayland session — the ghost-process bug that got the original desktop-session Waydroid decommissioned CANNOT recur, because the container's lifecycle is independent of the desktop session. Renders into a nested headless sway (wlroots) compositor exported over VNC via wayvnc — Debian bookworm's weston 10.0.1 rdp-backend.so was tried first but has a confirmed dead-connection bug (accepts the TCP handshake, never services the RDP protocol negotiation — isolated via fresh-container + single-client + thread-count + backtrace tests, 2026-07-04) and has no vnc-backend.so either; sway+wayvnc is the standard, actively-maintained combo for headless-Wayland VNC. The host connects with a VNC viewer in a window to see it (same GUI-bound up=boot+attach/down=teardown-on-close pattern as da_redroid, just a different display transport — scrcpy mirrors ADB/SurfaceFlinger, VNC mirrors a Wayland compositor). Exists because Waydroid's vendor image is memfd-based (no real /dev/ashmem needed) since 1.2.1+, so Chromium-based apps that crash under redroid (this host's mainline kernel has no ashmem) should work here — that's the whole point of this project."
  },
  "container": {
    "_doc": "Docker container knobs. image is OUR built image (Debian + waydroid + sway + wayvnc), never a public upstream tag — Waydroid has no official Docker image. base_image is what we build FROM.",
    "image": "ghcr.io/diegonmarcos/waydroid-container:13",
    "base_image": "debian:bookworm",
    "container_name": "waydroid-container",
    "data_volume": "/mnt/shared-lib/waydroid-container/data",
    "vnc_port": 5905,
    "vnc_addr": "127.0.0.1:5905",
    "_doc_vnc": "wayvnc 0.5.0 (Debian bookworm) binds this port with NO authentication by default (matches the prior RDP backend's no-app-login model) — port is bound to 127.0.0.1 only regardless (never exposed beyond the host). sway runs headless (WLR_BACKENDS=headless, no real GPU/input device) via seatd for the minimal libseat requirement wlroots still initializes even headless.",
    "width": 2304,
    "height": 1536,
    "cpu_cores": 7,
    "memory_reservation": "2g",
    "_doc_memory_reservation": "docker --memory-reservation (a SOFT limit, not a hard cap) — under host-wide memory pressure the kernel prefers reclaiming from cgroups that exceed their own reservation first, so this container's Android processes (system_server/zygote) get priority over other, unreserved cgroups instead of being starved by them. Confirmed necessary: on this host, concurrent unrelated processes pushed available memory under 1GB, and Android's own lmkd was crash-looping system_server every ~5s (dmesg: repeated 'Successfully killed process cgroup uid 1000') — a genuine host memory-contention bug, not a waydroid-container defect.",
    "stop_timeout_seconds": 30,
    "_doc_stop_timeout": "docker stop's grace period before SIGKILL. The entrypoint's SIGTERM trap does an ORDERED teardown (waydroid session stop -> waydroid container stop -> sunshine -> wayvnc -> sway -> seatd -> pulseaudio -> both dbus daemons) so every stack this container started actually exits cleanly rather than being cut off mid-shutdown by docker's 10s default — a SIGKILL mid-`waydroid container stop` can corrupt Android's persistent /data (the ghost-process class of bug that got the original desktop-session Waydroid decommissioned)."
  },
  "display": {
    "_doc": "Display architecture mode. 'native' (DEFAULT, the seamless UX): the HOST desktop's Wayland socket is bind-mounted into the container and Waydroid's hwcomposer connects to KDE DIRECTLY — Android appears as a native KDE window with ONE cursor, native touch/trackpad, native audio (host pulse socket), zero video-encode latency. This is exactly how bare-metal Waydroid-on-KDE works (docs.waydro.id core concepts: hwcomposer is a Wayland CLIENT of the user's compositor; the socket is bind-mounted into the Android container) — the Docker layer still owns the whole lifecycle, so the ghost-process class of bug cannot recur (docker stop kills everything). 'stream': the previous fully-headless stack (nested sway compositor + Sunshine VAAPI stream + Moonlight client) — remote-desktop UX with its inherent double-cursor/latency tradeoffs; kept for headless/remote use. Selected per-launch: `build.sh up` (native) / `build.sh up stream` / `build.sh up vnc`.",
    "mode": "native",
    "host_wayland_display": "wayland-0",
    "window_class": "Waydroid",
    "form_default": "tablet",
    "forms": {
      "_doc": "Launch FORMS — the same Android, two viewport shapes, picked per launch: `build.sh up [form]` (e.g. `up mobile`), default display.form_default. NATIVE MODE ONLY: width/height are persist.waydroid.width/height, read by Waydroid when the SESSION starts, so they size the real host window (there is no live resize — `wm size` retargets Android's logical display but leaves the window at its old size). Stream/vnc still size their headless sway output from container.width/height, baked into the container env at creation. MUST FIT THE LOGICAL SCREEN: these are LOGICAL pixels, and this panel is 2880x1920 at Plasma scale 1.25 => a 2304x1536 logical desktop (kscreen-doctor -o). tablet is exactly that, i.e. full-screen. mobile was 1080x2400 and overflowed the screen by ~900px vertically — 648x1440 is 20:9 with ~96px of headroom for panel + decoration. Re-check against `kscreen-doctor -o` Geometry if the scale or panel ever changes. The compositor scale is also why Android's physical display reads 1.25x these numbers, which is what `density` has to be computed against: smallestWidthDp = min(width,height)*1.25/(density/160) versus the sw600dp breakpoint. tablet 2880/(225/160)=1365dp -> tablet UI; mobile 810/(315/160)=411dp -> phone UI, the same dp width as a Pixel-class phone. Density is also the only global UI-scale knob available here: it sizes icon labels and text together, and Android's own font_scale setting is unreachable (`settings put` NPEs for uid 0). Lower density = more dp across the same pixels = smaller everything; 420 -> 315 is the 25% text reduction asked for on 2026-08-26. Shrinking the window WITHOUT raising density just gives a phone-shaped window still drawing tablet layouts, which is why this is a {width,height,density} triple and not a size. density is applied with `wm density` (WindowManager service) rather than `settings put` — the settings ContentProvider NPEs for uid 0, see entrypoint.sh. Switching form restarts the container: the resolution is fixed at session start.",
      "tablet": {
        "width": 2304,
        "height": 1536,
        "density": 225,
        "label": "Tablet"
      },
      "mobile": {
        "width": 648,
        "height": 1440,
        "density": 315,
        "label": "Phone"
      }
    }
  },
  "_doc_window_class": "The Waydroid native window's Wayland app-id / resourceClass on the host compositor (verified live via kdotool on KDE Plasma 6: class=Waydroid). Used two ways: KDE taskbar-pin matching (StartupWMClass in the HM desktop entry) and the native-mode GUI-bound lifecycle — build.sh polls `kdotool search --class <this>` and stops the container when the window closes (Wayland-native; X11 xdotool cannot see Wayland toplevels).",
  "stream": {
    "_doc": "PRIMARY display transport: Sunshine (in-container, VAAPI hardware H.264/HEVC encode on the passed-through Intel render node) + Moonlight (host client, hardware decode) — a full end-to-end GPU pipeline (Android hwcomposer EGL render -> sway GLES2 composite -> VAAPI encode -> Moonlight GPU decode), unlike VNC/RFB which is a CPU framebuffer-copy protocol with no video codec. VNC (wayvnc, container.vnc_*) is kept as the debug/fallback transport. Sunshine's Debian BOOKWORM build ended at v2025.122.141614 (2026 releases only ship trixie+) — pinned by sha256 for reproducibility. Web-UI credentials are GENERATED at first container start and persisted on the data volume (never in git); Moonlight pairing is automated by build.sh: it picks a random 4-digit PIN, runs `moonlight pair --pin`, and POSTs the same PIN to Sunshine's /api/pin with those generated creds. Pairing state persists on the data volume so it happens once.",
    "sunshine_deb_url": "https://github.com/LizardByte/Sunshine/releases/download/v2025.122.141614/sunshine-debian-bookworm-amd64.deb",
    "sunshine_deb_sha256": "4f08e5b9212fd087f2ad661850880a43bb5b262ebfc5305d6ba1c3f1a875ce56",
    "web_port": 47990,
    "app_name": "Desktop",
    "fps": 60,
    "client_flags": [
      "--display-mode",
      "windowed",
      "--absolute-mouse"
    ],
    "_doc_client_flags": "Moonlight is a GAME-streaming client: its defaults are exclusive fullscreen + relative (captured) mouse — the pointer gets trapped inside the window and the desktop underneath becomes unreachable (confirmed live, 2026-07-08). This is a desktop-app window, so (exact flag names read from moonlight-qt's commandlineparser.cpp): --display-mode windowed = a normal parallel KDE window (taskbar/desktop switching keep working); --absolute-mouse = remote-desktop pointer mode (moves freely in/out of the window, no capture, trackpad taps register as clicks). Touchscreen stays in its DEFAULT trackpad-emulation mode: --no-touchscreen-trackpad (native touch passthrough) was tried and is a known-broken path on Wayland — Sunshine registers the passthrough device as a drawing tablet (Sunshine#1828) and touch stopped working entirely (confirmed live). The double-cursor artifact of absolute-mouse mode is fixed SERVER-side (sway seat hide_cursor — moonlight-qt#1268/Sunshine#1940 document that hiding the host cursor is the accepted fix). Moonlight shortcuts: Ctrl+Alt+Shift+Q quit, Ctrl+Alt+Shift+M capture toggle.",
    "_doc_ports": "Sunshine's port family is derived from its base port (default 47989): HTTPS 47984, Web UI 47990, RTSP 48010, video/control UDP 47998-48000. Moonlight connects to the container's own bridge IP directly (same docker-userland-proxy-mangling avoidance as vnc_addr), so NO -p publishing is needed for streaming."
  },
  "waydroid": {
    "_doc": "waydroid CLI invocation knobs (manual, non-systemd — 'waydroid container start' + 'waydroid session start' are direct commands, no systemctl dependency, confirmed from docs.waydro.id/usage/waydroid-command-line-options). gpu_mode was removed 2026-07-08: waydroid 1.6.2's waydroid.cfg (vendor_type MAINLINE) has no gpu_mode key at all — the entrypoint's sed patch for it was a confirmed silent no-op (verified live: `cat waydroid.cfg` has no such field). GPU accel is whatever this vendor image defaults to; there is currently no user-facing toggle in this waydroid version.",
    "android_version": "13",
    "launcher_package": "com.android.launcher3",
    "launcher_home_activity": "com.android.launcher3/.uioverrides.QuickstepLauncher",
    "_doc_launcher_home_activity": "This vendor image's Launcher3 variant (LineageOS-branded 'Trebuchet', package com.android.launcher3) is only reliably launched as HOME by EXPLICIT component (`am start -n`), never by the ambiguous `-c android.intent.category.HOME` category intent — confirmed live 2026-07-09: once apps.list installs a second/third app that also declares the HOME category (Cloud-SuperApp, Mako — both legitimately launcher-capable), `-c HOME` triggers Android's disambiguation resolver ('Use Trebuchet as Home' / Just once / Always), which BLOCKS rendering until a real tap answers it — no error, no crash, just a stuck resolver silently covering the correctly-seeded home screen (the favorites DB was verified correct on disk the whole time; only the resolver was wrong). `cmd package set-home-activity` alone does not prevent this once it's already been triggered by an earlier ambiguous `-c HOME` call — the fix is to never issue an ambiguous HOME intent in the first place.",
    "launcher_db_glob": "com.android.launcher3/databases/launcher_*.db",
    "container_tmp": "/data/local/tmp",
    "grant_all_permissions": true,
    "hotseat_container": -101,
    "workspace_container": -100,
    "item_type_application": 0,
    "item_type_folder": 2,
    "launch_flags": "0x10200000",
    "_doc_provisioning": "Reintroduced 2026-07-09 from the decommissioned da_waydroid-apps project (z_archive/da_waydroid-apps) — the same declarative apps/launcher/theme model, ported to run INSIDE this Docker container instead of against a bare-metal Waydroid install. APKs are baked into the image at `build.sh build` time (bundle-context step `apps-fetch`: `nix build src/apps#apks`, reproducible via src/apps/apps.lock.json's pinned versionCode+sha256 — COPY'd into /opt/apps/<pkg>.apk). Provisioning (install + appops grants + launcher layout + theme) runs ONCE per container lifetime from entrypoint.sh after Android first reaches boot_completed=1, guarded by a marker file on the persistent /var/lib/waydroid volume so re-boots of the same container never re-run it. `grant_all_permissions` (-g on pm install) auto-grants every declared runtime (dangerous) permission for every app — no per-app install prompts."
  },
  "apps": {
    "_doc": "Declarative app set. Every entry: package (Android applicationId), label, group (free-form tag, used only for docs/status — layout membership is explicit in `launcher` below, never derived from group), source ({type: fdroid|github, url? for github}), optional appops (special app-ops beyond the -g runtime-permission grant, applied via `cmd appops set <pkg> <op> allow`), optional full_perms (true = this is one of Diego's OWN apps — gets the full extra appops bundle in provisioning.full_perms_appops, not just its own declared appops list). Source of truth for src/apps/apps.lock.json (pinned versionCode/sha256, regenerate via the archived lock logic if this list changes).",
    "full_perms_appops": [
      "REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES",
      "SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW",
      "WRITE_SETTINGS",
      "MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE"
    ],
    "_doc_full_perms": "The extra app-ops bundle granted to every app with full_perms:true (Diego's own constellation apps: SuperApp/CloudNav/Comms/IDE) — these are the app-ops Android gates behind explicit user action outside the normal runtime-permission dialog (install-other-APKs, draw-over-other-apps, modify-system-settings, all-files access). Combined with grant_all_permissions (-g, all declared dangerous runtime perms), this is genuinely 'full perms' for these apps; third-party apps only get their own declared `appops` list (usually none) plus -g.",
